  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>Why standard EMDR stalls</h2>
      <p>If the trauma is a single event and your nervous system is otherwise stable, standard
      protocol often works well and quickly. If the trauma is developmental, something else is
      true: parts of you organized around keeping that material away, and they did it for years,
      competently, because at the time it was the only thing keeping you functional.</p>
      <p>Ask those parts to stand aside for a protocol and they will do what they have always
      done. The processing floods, or it flatlines, or you find yourself unable to bring the
      target to mind at all. From the outside it looks like EMDR not working. From the inside it
      is a protector doing its job.</p>
      <h3>What changes with parts work</h3>
      <p>Internal Family Systems gives us language for that. Rather than pushing past the
      protector, we get to know it. What is it afraid will happen if you go near this? What does
      it need in place first? What has it been carrying, and for how long?</p>
      <p>Reem's emphasis is phases one and two, history-taking and preparation, because that is
      where stalled EMDR actually stalls. Build enough stability and enough internal agreement
      there, and the later phases stop being a fight.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>What this looks like in session</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Naming the parts in the room: the one that wants to heal, the one that wants to
        leave, the one that thinks this is self-indulgent.</li>
        <li>Unblending, so you can look at a part rather than speak as it.</li>
        <li>Asking permission from protectors before any target gets processed.</li>
        <li>Resourcing and stabilization that a protective part actually trusts.</li>
        <li>Slowing or pausing processing when a part objects, and treating that objection as
        information rather than resistance.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>It tends to fit when</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>EMDR has been tried before and went nowhere, or went badly.</li>
        <li>You flood, dissociate or go blank when you approach the material.</li>
        <li>Part of you wants this and part of you really does not.</li>
        <li>The trauma is developmental and layered rather than a single event.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Reem completed steps one through three of IFS-informed EMDR training in 2025, is EMDR
      certified and an EMDRIA Approved Consultant, and
      <a href="/for-therapists/emdr-consultation/">consults with other therapists</a> on exactly
      this problem.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

