<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about IFS-informed EMDR</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What is Internal Family Systems?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>A model that treats the mind as made up of parts, each with its own job and its own
      fears, around a core self that is not a part. Protective parts manage pain; exiled parts
      carry it. The work is relational: you get to know a part rather than overriding it.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is this the same as full IFS therapy?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. This is EMDR practiced with IFS awareness, not IFS as a standalone treatment. The
      parts work is in service of making EMDR usable for developmental trauma, particularly in
      the preparation phases.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>My EMDR flooded me last time. Will that happen again?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It is the specific thing this approach exists to prevent. Flooding usually means
      processing started before the parts holding the material had agreed to it, or before there
      was enough resourcing for them to trust. Both are preparation problems, and preparation is
      where the emphasis sits here.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How is this different from just going slower?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Pace helps, but the obstacle is not usually speed. A protector that believes something
      catastrophic follows from touching this material will object at any speed. What changes
      things is finding out what it is protecting and what it needs, which is a different
      conversation rather than a slower one.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I have to believe in parts for this to work?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Parts language is a working model, not a metaphysics. Plenty of people use it as a
      convenient way of describing conflicting impulses they already recognize, and the work
      proceeds the same either way.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
